m03-l14-first-sketch-blink.rawMarkdown:
## 1. The Anatomy of an Arduino Sketch
In desktop software, a program has a `main()` function that starts, runs a task, and exits to the operating system.

In embedded robotics, **a microcontroller never exits!** If the code stopped, your robot would stop steering or regulating motor power.

Every Arduino program (called a **sketch**) is written in C++ and organized around two mandatory functions:

```cpp
void setup() {
  // Runs ONCE when board powers on or resets
}

void loop() {
  // Runs REPEATEDLY forever
}
```

<Callout type="safety" title="Pin Current Limit Safety">
  Each ATmega328P output pin can supply a maximum of 20mA safely (absolute max 40mA). Never connect an LED or motor directly to a digital pin without a current-limiting resistor or driver transistor. Exceeding current limits overheats and permanently burns the internal silicon driver.
</Callout>

## 2. Configuring Pins with `pinMode()`
By default, microcontroller pins wake up as high-impedance inputs to protect connected circuits. Before sending electricity out to an LED or transistor, we must configure the pin driver:

```cpp
pinMode(13, OUTPUT);
```
- Pin 13 is tied to an onboard amber LED (marked **L**) on the Arduino Uno PCB.
- `OUTPUT` switches the internal CMOS transistor pair so the pin can source or sink current.

## 3. Driving Voltages with `digitalWrite()`
Digital pins on the ATmega328P can only output two states:
- `HIGH`: Connects the pin to $+5\text{ V}$.
- `LOW`: Connects the pin to $\text{GND}$ ($0\text{ V}$).

```cpp
digitalWrite(13, HIGH); // 5V -> LED turns ON
delay(1000);            // Pause execution for 1000 milliseconds (1 second)
digitalWrite(13, LOW);  // 0V -> LED turns OFF
delay(1000);            // Pause for 1 second
```

## 4. The Complete Blink Sketch
```cpp
const int LED_PIN = 13;

void setup() {
  pinMode(LED_PIN, OUTPUT);
}

void loop() {
  digitalWrite(LED_PIN, HIGH);
  delay(1000);
  digitalWrite(LED_PIN, LOW);
  delay(1000);
}
```

When `loop()` reaches the closing brace `}`, the MCU immediately restarts at the first line of `loop()`, creating a steady 0.5 Hz blink!

## 5. Summary & Best Practices
- Define pin numbers as `const int` at the top of your file to prevent typos.
- Only run one-time hardware configuration inside `setup()`.
- `delay(ms)` stops the CPU completely; in later lessons, we will learn non-blocking timers using `millis()`.

m03-l15-digital-io-and-pullups.rawMarkdown:
## 1. The Mystery of the Phantom Button
Imagine wiring a pushbutton between $5\text{ V}$ and digital pin 2. When you press the button, pin 2 reads `HIGH` ($5\text{ V}$).

What does pin 2 read when you let go?

You might guess `LOW` ($0\text{ V}$). But if you test it, the pin rapidly and erratically flickers between 0 and 1! Even waving your hand near the wire changes the reading. Why?

<Callout type="safety" title="GPIO Voltage Protection">
  Digital input pins must never be exposed to voltages higher than VCC (+5.5V) or negative voltages below GND. Excessive external voltage blows the internal clamping diodes and damages the chip. Operate exclusively at low-voltage 5V DC logic levels.
</Callout>

## 2. The Floating Pin Phenomenon
Microcontroller input pins have enormous internal input impedance (over $100\text{ M}\Omega$). When the switch is open, the pin is connected to **nothing**—it is **floating**.

Just like static electricity on a balloon attracts your hair, electromagnetic waves from mains wiring, fluorescent lights, and static charge in the air induce tiny voltages on the floating pin, causing unpredictable false triggers!

## 3. The Solution: Pull-up and Pull-down Resistors
To guarantee a stable reading when the switch is open, we must tie the pin to a known voltage rail through a weak resistor:
- **Pull-down Resistor (External 10k to GND):** Pin defaults to 0V (`LOW`). Pressing button brings it to 5V (`HIGH`).
- **Pull-up Resistor (Weak Resistor to 5V):** Pin defaults to 5V (`HIGH`). Pressing button shorts the pin to GND (`LOW`).

## 4. Secret Hardware Superpower: `INPUT_PULLUP`
Wiring external 10k resistors on a breadboard takes up space and wires. The designers of the ATmega328P included internal $20\text{ k}\Omega$ to $50\text{ k}\Omega$ pull-up resistors inside the silicon chip itself!

You can turn them on with a single line of code:
```cpp
pinMode(2, INPUT_PULLUP);
```

### Wiring with `INPUT_PULLUP`:
Connect one terminal of your momentary button to **Pin 2**, and the other terminal directly to **GND**. No external resistors needed!

### Active-LOW Logic:
- **Button Released (Open):** Internal pull-up pulls pin to $5\text{ V}$ $\rightarrow$ `digitalRead(2) == HIGH`
- **Button Pressed (Closed):** Current flows to ground $\rightarrow$ `digitalRead(2) == LOW`

```cpp
const int BUTTON_PIN = 2;
const int LED_PIN = 13;

void setup() {
  pinMode(BUTTON_PIN, INPUT_PULLUP);
  pinMode(LED_PIN, OUTPUT);
}

void loop() {
  int buttonState = digitalRead(BUTTON_PIN);

  if (buttonState == LOW) {
    // Button is physically pressed!
    digitalWrite(LED_PIN, HIGH);
  } else {
    digitalWrite(LED_PIN, LOW);
  }
}
```

## 5. Summary & Best Practices
- Never leave a digital input pin floating.
- Standard robotics practice is using `INPUT_PULLUP` with buttons wired to GND.
- Remember the inverted logic: `LOW` means PRESSED, `HIGH` means RELEASED.

m03-l17-pwm-analog-output.rawMarkdown:
## 1. Hook: Can a Digital Pin Output 2.5 Volts?
We know digital pins can only output $0\text{ V}$ or $5\text{ V}$.

What if you want to dim an LED to half brightness, or drive a robot motor at 50% cruising speed? Does the microcontroller have an analog valve to output $2.5\text{ V}$?

No! Instead, it uses a brilliant trick of physics: **Pulse Width Modulation (PWM)**.

<Callout type="safety" title="PWM Motor Drive Isolation">
  While PWM can control DC motor speed, never wire a motor coil directly to an Arduino PWM pin! Motors draw high current and generate inductive flyback spikes. Always buffer PWM signals through a dedicated driver transistor, MOSFET, or H-bridge IC.
</Callout>

## 2. What is Pulse Width Modulation?
PWM switches a digital output pin between $5\text{ V}$ (HIGH) and $0\text{ V}$ (LOW) hundreds of times per second (approx $490\text{ Hz}$ or $980\text{ Hz}$ on the Arduino Uno).

Because it switches so fast (faster than human persistence of vision or motor coil inductance can react), the connected device responds to the **time-averaged voltage**:

$$V_{\text{avg}} = \text{Duty Cycle} \times V_{\text{max}}$$

### Duty Cycle ($D$):
The percentage of time the signal is HIGH during each repeating period:
$$D = \frac{T_{\text{on}}}{T_{\text{on}} + T_{\text{off}}} \times 100\%$$

- **0% Duty Cycle:** Pin is permanently LOW ($0\text{ V}$).
- **25% Duty Cycle:** HIGH for $1/4$ of the cycle $\rightarrow V_{\text{avg}} = 0.25 \times 5\text{ V} = 1.25\text{ V}$.
- **50% Duty Cycle:** HIGH for $1/2$ of the cycle $\rightarrow V_{\text{avg}} = 0.50 \times 5\text{ V} = 2.50\text{ V}$.
- **100% Duty Cycle:** Pin is permanently HIGH ($5\text{ V}$).

## 3. The `analogWrite()` Function
In Arduino code, PWM is controlled using `analogWrite()`.

Despite the name, `analogWrite()` is NOT true analog; it sets an **8-bit timer compare register**:
$$\text{Range: } 0 \text{ to } 255 \quad (2^8 = 256 \text{ levels})$$

- `analogWrite(pin, 0)` $\rightarrow 0\%$ duty ($0\text{ V}$)
- `analogWrite(pin, 64)` $\rightarrow 25\%$ duty ($1.25\text{ V}$)
- `analogWrite(pin, 128)` $\rightarrow 50\%$ duty ($2.50\text{ V}$)
- `analogWrite(pin, 255)` $\rightarrow 100\%$ duty ($5.0\text{ V}$)

### Which Pins Support PWM?
Look closely at the Arduino Uno board silkscreen. Certain digital pins have a tilde (~) symbol:
**Pins 3, 5, 6, 9, 10, and 11.** Only these specific pins are wired to internal hardware timer output compare channels!

## 4. Example: Smooth LED Breathing Fader
```cpp
const int LED_PIN = 9; // Pin 9 supports PWM (~)

void setup() {
  pinMode(LED_PIN, OUTPUT);
}

void loop() {
  // Fade in from dark to bright
  for (int b = 0; b <= 255; b++) {
    analogWrite(LED_PIN, b);
    delay(5);
  }

  // Fade out from bright to dark
  for (int b = 255; b >= 0; b--) {
    analogWrite(LED_PIN, b);
    delay(5);
  }
}
```

## 5. Summary
- PWM mimics continuous analog voltage by switching digital outputs on and off at high frequency.
- `analogWrite()` takes values from $0$ to $255$.
- Available strictly on hardware timer pins marked with `~` (3, 5, 6, 9, 10, 11).

m03-l18-serial-monitor-debugging.rawMarkdown:
## 1. Hook: Coding with a Blindfold
When writing code on your computer, you can easily inspect variables or print messages to the screen.

When your code is running inside a sealed silicon microcontroller chip on a robot rolling across the floor, how do you know what it is thinking? If a motor fails to spin, is the sensor reading wrong, the battery dead, or the code stuck in an infinite loop?

The answer is the **Serial Monitor**!

<Callout type="safety" title="USB & Bench Safety">
  When connecting your computer via USB to an Arduino mounted on a robot, ensure the robot's wheels are raised off the desk surface so unexpected motor commands cannot drive the robot off the edge. Disconnect high-current external battery packs when re-programming if not using a shared common ground.
</Callout>

## 2. What is UART?
**UART** stands for Universal Asynchronous Receiver-Transmitter. It is one of the oldest, most reliable digital communication protocols in computing:
- **TX (Transmit):** Sends outgoing bits of data.
- **RX (Receive):** Listens for incoming bits of data.
- **Asynchronous:** There is no shared clock line! Both devices agree in advance on the transmission speed: the **baud rate**.

On the Arduino Uno, digital Pin 0 is **RX** and Pin 1 is **TX**, connected directly to the USB interface chip.

## 3. Baud Rates: Timing Must Match
Baud rate is measured in **symbols (bits) per second**:
- Common speeds: `9600`, `19200`, `57600`, `115200`.
- At $9600\text{ baud}$, each bit takes $1 / 9600 \approx 104.2\text{ }\mu\text{s}$.
- If the Arduino transmits at $9600\text{ baud}$ but your computer is listening at $115200\text{ baud}$, the incoming characters will appear as garbled alien gibberish (`⸮⸮x⸮~`)!

## 4. Basic Serial Code
```cpp
void setup() {
  // Start serial communications at 9600 baud
  Serial.begin(9600);
  Serial.println("--- RoboForge Boot Sequence Initiated ---");
}

void loop() {
  int sensorValue = analogRead(A0);

  // Print on one line without a carriage return
  Serial.print("Sensor reading: ");

  // Print value followed by a new line
  Serial.println(sensorValue);

  delay(500);
}
```

### The Difference Between `print()` and `println()`:
- `Serial.print("Hello")`: Sends the text and leaves the cursor on the same line.
- `Serial.println("World")`: Sends the text followed by `\r\n` (Carriage Return + Newline), advancing to the next row.

## 5. Summary & Debugging Rules
- Always initialize `Serial.begin(baud)` in `setup()`.
- Ensure your terminal baud rate matches your code.
- Avoid placing thousands of `Serial.print()` statements inside tight loops without delays; serial transmission takes time and can slow down time-critical motor loops!

m03-l19-code-logic-and-state-machines.rawMarkdown:
## 1. The Danger of `delay()`
In early lessons, we used `delay(1000)` to pause our sketch.

Imagine an autonomous robot driving toward a staircase. Your code is currently running `delay(2000)` for a turn. During those two full seconds, the CPU is completely frozen in a busy loop!
- It cannot read the cliff sensor.
- It cannot detect the emergency stop button.
- The robot tumbles off the edge!

Roboticists replace blocking delays with **non-blocking cooperative timers** using `millis()`.

<Callout type="safety" title="Fail-Safe State Machine Design">
  Always include an explicit emergency stop state (e.g. STATE_EMERGENCY_STOP) in your robot's state machine. In unhandled edge cases or unexpected sensor timeouts, the default fail-safe behavior must immediately kill motor power and stop mechanical motion.
</Callout>

## 2. The `millis()` Stopwatch
`millis()` returns the number of milliseconds that have elapsed since the Arduino board powered on or was reset:
- Returns an `unsigned long` (rolls over after approximately 50 days of continuous operation).
- Like checking your wristwatch: instead of standing still waiting for an alarm, you check the current time against your target time while continuing other work!

```cpp
unsigned long previousMillis = 0;
const long interval = 1000;
int ledState = LOW;

void loop() {
  unsigned long currentMillis = millis();

  // Has 1 second elapsed since the last toggle?
  if (currentMillis - previousMillis >= interval) {
    previousMillis = currentMillis; // Reset stopwatch marker
    ledState = (ledState == LOW) ? HIGH : LOW;
    digitalWrite(13, ledState);
  }

  // The CPU can read sensors and process buttons HERE without waiting!
}
```

## 3. Finite State Machines (FSM)
A robot cannot perform every action at once. It moves through distinct **states**:
1. `STATE_PATROL`: Drive forward in search of targets.
2. `STATE_OBSTACLE`: Stop, reverse, and steer clear of a wall.
3. `STATE_LOW_BATTERY`: Halt motors and sound a warning beeper.

### Building an FSM with `enum` and `switch`:
```cpp
enum RobotState {
  STATE_PATROL,
  STATE_OBSTACLE,
  STATE_CELEBRATE
};

RobotState currentState = STATE_PATROL;

void loop() {
  switch (currentState) {
    case STATE_PATROL:
      // Drive motors forward
      if (checkWallSensor() < 15) {
        currentState = STATE_OBSTACLE; // State transition
      }
      break;

    case STATE_OBSTACLE:
      // Pivot turn left
      if (turnCompleted()) {
        currentState = STATE_PATROL;   // Return to patrol
      }
      break;

    case STATE_CELEBRATE:
      // Spin in a victory circle
      break;
  }
}
```

## 4. Summary & Engineering Wisdom
- Never use `delay()` in production robotics code.
- Use `millis() - previousTime >= interval` for periodic tasks.
- Structure complex autonomous behavior using an explicit state machine with named `enum` states.

m03-l20-sensor-decisions-hysteresis.rawMarkdown:
## 1. The Chattering Headlight Problem
Suppose you program an automated night-light robot:
```cpp
if (lightReading < 500) {
  turnLightON();
} else {
  turnLightOFF();
}
```

At dusk, as ambient daylight hovers right around $500$, random electrical noise in the sensor wire causes readings like:
`499, 501, 498, 502, 500, 499...`

Your light will rapidly flicker on and off dozens of times per second! In a motor system, this rapid switching (called **hunting** or **chatter**) causes violent mechanical shuddering, wasted power, and burned motor coils.

<Callout type="safety" title="Chatter & Thermal Hazard Prevention">
  Rapidly toggling high-current loads (like DC motors, solenoids, or mechanical relays) on and off due to sensor noise creates massive voltage transients and excessive coil heating. Always implement hysteresis and rate-limiting delays to protect hardware from thermal runaway.
</Callout>

## 2. Solution 1: Software Low-Pass Filtering (EMA)
Before making decisions on a raw ADC reading, smooth out the high-frequency noise spikes with an **Exponential Moving Average (EMA)** filter:

$$Y_n = (1 - \alpha) \cdot Y_{n-1} + \alpha \cdot X_n$$
- $X_n$: New raw sensor reading.
- $Y_n$: Filtered output.
- $\alpha$: Smoothing factor ($0.1$ is very smooth, $0.5$ is more responsive).

```cpp
float filteredLight = 500.0;
const float ALPHA = 0.15;

void loop() {
  int raw = analogRead(A0);
  filteredLight = (1.0 - ALPHA) * filteredLight + ALPHA * raw;
}
```

## 3. Solution 2: Hysteresis (Dual Thresholds)
The definitive cure for chatter is **hysteresis** (the operating principle behind the hardware Schmitt Trigger and home thermostats):

Instead of a single threshold at $500$, use **two separate thresholds**:
- **Upper Threshold ($550$):** Light turns OFF only when ambient light climbs safely above 550.
- **Lower Threshold ($450$):** Light turns ON only when ambient light drops cleanly below 450.
- **Deadband ($450$ to $550$):** The system holds its previous state and ignores fluctuations!

```cpp
const int THRESHOLD_LOW = 450;
const int THRESHOLD_HIGH = 550;
bool headlightActive = false;

void loop() {
  int light = analogRead(A0);

  if (headlightActive && light > THRESHOLD_HIGH) {
    headlightActive = false;
    digitalWrite(LED_PIN, LOW); // It is definitely bright daylight
  } 
  else if (!headlightActive && light < THRESHOLD_LOW) {
    headlightActive = true;
    digitalWrite(LED_PIN, HIGH); // It is definitely dark
  }
}
```

## 4. Summary & Best Practices
- Never use a single sharp cutoff boundary for noisy physical sensors.
- Use an exponential filter ($\alpha \approx 0.1$) to eliminate ADC jitter.
- Always implement a hysteresis band to prevent motor and relay chatter.

